**Proof.** At time $r$, the target coordinate indexed by $t(c)$ copies the source coordinate indexed by $s(c)$ if $r+\epsilon<d_{t(c)}$, and is zero otherwise. Unmatched target coordinates are zero. Injectivity of the occurrence embeddings makes this well-defined even for tied intervals. If the copied target has not been born, the birth bound forces the source coordinate to vanish. If a target coordinate survives a later shifted time, its death bound forces the corresponding source coordinate to survive the unshifted later time; this proves every naturality square. Construct the reverse maps symmetrically. A matched source coordinate surviving $r+2\epsilon$ has its intermediate target surviving $r+\epsilon$ by the other death inequality, so the reverse-after-forward composite is its actual structure-map coordinate. The forward-after-reverse argument uses the opposite death inequality. An unmatched coordinate either is not born at $r$ or is dead by $r+2\epsilon$. These arguments include short or disjoint pairs, exact length $2\epsilon$, $\epsilon=0$, empty families and essential intervals; no separate overlap assumption is required. Natural isomorphism conjugation transfers both composite equations to the real extensions. For the forward direction of Theorem 5.1, the second interleaving equation at $r+\epsilon$ gives the range inclusion into $f_{r+2\epsilon}$ for the target $N(\epsilon)$. Its translated birth and death endpoints allow Theorem 4.8 to apply to this same map. Undoing the translation gives both endpoint bounds. The corresponding feasible sets of finite nonnegative radii, embedded in the extended nonnegative reals, are equal, so their infima are equal even when both feasible sets are empty.

